# Proof: No Limit Cycles in the Two-Species Negative Feedback System

## Setup and steady state

Consider
$$\dot u_1 = f(u_2) - k_1 u_1,\qquad \dot u_2 = u_1 - k_2 u_2,$$
with $f>0$, $f'<0$, and $k_1,k_2>0$.

A steady state $(u_1^*,u_2^*)$ satisfies $k_1 u_1^* = f(u_2^*)$ and $u_1^* = k_2 u_2^*$, i.e. $k_1 k_2 u_2^* = f(u_2^*)$. Since $f>0$ is decreasing and $k_1 k_2 u_2$ is increasing, a unique positive equilibrium exists (under mild growth conditions on $f$; its existence is not essential to the argument below).

## Jacobian and local stability

The Jacobian at any point is
$$J = \begin{pmatrix} -k_1 & f'(u_2) \\ 1 & -k_2 \end{pmatrix}.$$

- $\operatorname{tr} J = -(k_1+k_2) < 0$ (constant, independent of operating point),
- $\det J = k_1 k_2 - f'(u_2) > k_1 k_2 > 0$ (since $f'<0$).

Both eigenvalues therefore have strictly negative real parts at every equilibrium; the steady state is **always locally asymptotically stable**. In particular the trace can never cross zero, so **no Hopf bifurcation** is possible — the only generic route to a limit cycle in a 2D smooth system is unavailable.

## Bendixson–Dulac criterion: exclusion of all closed orbits

The divergence of the vector field is
$$\frac{\partial \dot u_1}{\partial u_1} + \frac{\partial \dot u_2}{\partial u_2} = -k_1 - k_2 < 0,$$
a **constant, strictly negative** quantity on all of $\mathbb{R}^2$ (a simply connected region). By the **Bendixson criterion** (Dulac with $B\equiv 1$), a $C^1$ vector field whose divergence has constant sign and is not identically zero on a simply connected domain admits **no closed orbits** whatsoever.

Hence there are no periodic trajectories — and consequently no limit cycle oscillations — for any choice of $f$ (with $f>0$, $f'<0$) and any $k_1,k_2>0$.

## Connection to the secant criterion

This is the $n=2$ instance of the general negative-feedback oscillation theorem (the *secant criterion*): an $n$-variable monotone negative feedback loop can oscillate only if the loop gain exceeds $\sec(\pi/n)$. For $n=2$, $\sec(\pi/2)=+\infty$, so the required gain is infinite — i.e. **oscillation is impossible** for any finite parameters. Two variables are simply too few to sustain a negative-feedback oscillation; a third variable (providing additional phase lag) is necessary.

## Conclusion

Under the stated hypotheses ($f(u_2)>0$, $f'(u_2)<0$, $k_1,k_2>0$), the two-species negative feedback system **admits no limit cycle oscillations under any conditions**. The reasons are decisive and independent of the detailed shape of $f$:

1. The divergence $-(k_1+k_2)$ is constant and strictly negative, so by the Bendixson–Dulac criterion no closed orbit can exist in the plane.
2. The trace $-(k_1+k_2)<0$ can never vanish, ruling out a Hopf bifurcation.
3. This is the $n=2$ case of the secant criterion, where the critical gain $\sec(\pi/2)=\infty$ is unattainable.

Oscillation requires at least three interacting species (or an explicit delay) to supply sufficient phase lag around the feedback loop.

